  "background": "Hosea prophesies primarily to the northern kingdom of Israel, often called Ephraim, during the eighth century BC as Assyrian pressure rises and political stability deteriorates. The superscription places his ministry across the reigns of several Judean kings and Jeroboam II of Israel, while the book’s political references fit the turbulent decades before Samaria’s fall in 722 BC.\n\nHosea’s rhetoric is intentionally shocking. Marriage, sexual infidelity, parent-child love, agriculture, animals, sickness, warfare, and courtroom accusation become prophetic metaphors. Because the marriage imagery uses an ancient patriarchal and honor-shame world, readers must distinguish the metaphor’s covenant point from any supposed permission to shame, strip, control, stalk, deprive, own, or endanger a spouse. Hosea is an intra-Israel covenant critique and must not become an antisemitic caricature of Jewish people or a slogan that God simply discarded Israel for the church.",
